# Third-party corpora do not go into git; fetch scripts obtain them on demand.
# The one exception is pdg.sqlite (see below).

# PDG sqlite: 7,593,984 bytes (about 7.24 MiB). This case pins PDG 2024, while the
# online edition has already moved to an incompatible 2026 (the 2024/2025 URLs now
# return 404), so this one file is committed to the repository as a re-runnable
# corpus; every other *.sqlite stays out of git.
*.sqlite
!pdg/pdg.sqlite

# llama.cpp official builds: about 2.1 GB once unpacked
# — they must never go into git. Asset names are pinned in
#   llama/corpus_manifest.json under asset_pins and fetched/unpacked by
#   llama/fetch_corpus.py.
llama/llama-b*/
llama/downloads/
*.zip

# ★ Note: llama/corpus_manifest.json **must** go into git.
#   It records measured artifact-side facts (what exists, how big, what the
#   probes read); even without the corpus a reader can check readout consistency.
#   It cannot replace re-running the probes — which is why fetch_corpus.py ships too.

# port/: build artifacts of the two ports, produced on the spot with rustc; not in git
*.exe
*.pdb
port/impl_rust/port_rust

# malecns: two feather files, 56 MB, fetched live from a GCS direct link;
# SHA256 anchored in test_aligned.py
malecns/*.feather

# k8s: two official OpenAPI snapshots, 6.2 MB, fetched live from jsDelivr/mirrors;
# SHA256 anchored in ASSETS in k8s/fetch_data.py (and re-verified by test_aligned.py).
k8s/k8s-*.json

# ★ Note: k8s/corpus_manifest.json **must** go into git (same reason as
#   llama/malecns) — it records measured corpus-side facts (hash, size, which
#   mirror, which path is already broken). It cannot replace re-running
#   aligned.py — which is why fetch_data.py ships too.
